<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">River Trent</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trento">Trent</a> is the third-longest river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, nearly 300 kilometres long, flowing from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/staffordshire">Staffordshire</a> moorlands through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midlands">Midlands</a> to join the Ouse and form the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humber">Humber</a> estuary. Its upper valley was the heartland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> kingdom of Mercia, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was regarded as the dividing line between northern and southern England.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/river-trent.jpg" alt="River Trent" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andrew Abbott · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:River_Trent_-_geograph.org.uk_-_6470987.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trento">Trent</a> rises in the Staffordshire Moorlands, near Biddulph Moor, and flows south past Stoke-on-Trent, Stone, Rugeley, Burton upon Trent, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nottingham">Nottingham</a>, Newark-on-Trent and Gainsborough. At Trent Falls it joins the Yorkshire Ouse to form the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humber">Humber</a> estuary, which has often been characterised as the boundary between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midlands">Midlands</a> and the North. Its largest tributary is the Tame, which supplies nearly a quarter of its flow, followed by the Derwent, Soar, Dove and Sow.</p><p>The name is possibly Romano-British and meant strongly flooding. Another derivation, from Trisantona, gives great thoroughfare. Izaak Walton's explanation of 1653, that the river was named for thirty kinds of fish, is almost certainly wrong.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The river changed its channel during historic times, and has been compared to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> in this respect. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/repton">Repton</a> an abandoned channel was once the main navigable route until the river moved to a more northerly course in the 18th century, and at Hemington archaeologists found a medieval bridge across another abandoned channel. Before the mid-18th century there were only four bridges downstream of the Tame, at Burton, Swarkestone, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nottingham">Nottingham</a> and Newark, all first built by 1204, and over thirty ferries.</p><p>Fishing along the river goes back to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> period, to which a possible fish weir at Hemington may belong. Domesday records mills and fisheries along it, and in the 12th century landlords at Burton upon <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trento">Trent</a> were paid in salmon in place of rent. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nottingham">Nottingham</a> seems to have been the ancient head of navigation until the Restoration. The Trent Navigation Act 1773 authorised locks at Newark, and tolls fell from £11,344 in 1839 to £3,111 in 1855 as railways took the traffic. The Ministry of Transport took over the navigation on 24 September 1920, and Neville Chamberlain opened Hazelford lock on 25 June 1926.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trento">Trent</a> also marked the boundary between northern and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. Royal forests had different justice in eyre north and south of it, the medieval Council of the North's jurisdiction began there, and Oxford University divided its students into northern and southern nations by it. Industrial growth polluted the river: a salmon fishery taking some 3,000 fish a year in the late 1880s had fallen to 100 a decade later.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The river is known for flooding, with a well-documented flood history of some 900 years, and its tidal lower reaches have a bore called the Trent Aegir, up to 1.5 metres high, which travels as far as Gainsborough. Twelve large power stations were built along its banks from the 1940s, using its cooling water, and at one time supplied a quarter of the electricity of the United Kingdom, giving the valley the name Megawatt Valley. The Trent Valley Way, a long-distance footpath, was created in 1998.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “River Trent” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
